<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?>
<!--
  Elevation is asked for up front, by the manifest, so that Windows shows the
  consent prompt before the program starts instead of the program discovering
  halfway through that it cannot write. Registering an ASIO driver writes to
  HKEY_CLASSES_ROOT and to HKLM\SOFTWARE\ASIO, and the driver file goes into
  %ProgramFiles%; none of that is possible without elevation.

  The program also checks at run time and stops with a clear message if it finds
  itself unelevated, which is what happens if this manifest is ever stripped.

  DPI AWARENESS, and why it is PerMonitorV2.

  Without a declaration Windows treats a process as unaware, scales its window as
  a bitmap and the result is soft, smeared text. On the machine this was written
  for that is not a theoretical complaint: it is a high density screen and the
  window would look blurred on it, which is precisely the "something cheap is
  running" impression the window exists to remove.

  PerMonitorV2 is asked for because it is the only mode where Windows also scales
  the CAPTION, the system menu and the dialogs to match, and where the window is
  told about a DPI change through WM_DPICHANGED with the rectangle it should move
  to. gui.cpp handles that message: it rebuilds the fonts, decodes the device
  photograph again at the new pixel size, and lays the page out again. Per monitor
  v1 is listed after it as the fallback for a Windows that does not know v2, and
  the older <dpiAware>true</dpiAware> is kept as the last fallback, which gets
  system awareness - correct on one monitor and stale after a DPI change, which is
  still far better than a bitmap stretch.

  COMMON CONTROLS 6. Without this dependency the buttons and the progress bar are
  drawn in the Windows 95 style, which is exactly the look being removed. It
  changes the appearance of controls and nothing else; the driver's own
  registration code runs in this process and does not touch a control.

  Deliberately NOT set here:
    - activeCodePage UTF-8. The driver's own registration code uses the ANSI
      registry API, and it runs inside this process. Changing the process code
      page would change how it stores the driver path.
    - longPathAware. It only takes effect together with a machine wide policy,
      so relying on it would make behaviour depend on the machine.
-->
<assembly xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v1" manifestVersion="1.0">
  <assemblyIdentity type="win32"
                    name="BCD3000.AsioDriver.Installer"
                    version="1.0.0.0"
                    processorArchitecture="*"/>
  <trustInfo xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v3">
    <security>
      <requestedPrivileges>
        <requestedExecutionLevel level="requireAdministrator" uiAccess="false"/>
      </requestedPrivileges>
    </security>
  </trustInfo>
  <application xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v3">
    <windowsSettings>
      <dpiAware xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2005/WindowsSettings">true</dpiAware>
      <dpiAwareness xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2016/WindowsSettings">PerMonitorV2,PerMonitor</dpiAwareness>
    </windowsSettings>
  </application>
  <dependency>
    <dependentAssembly>
      <assemblyIdentity type="win32"
                        name="Microsoft.Windows.Common-Controls"
                        version="6.0.0.0"
                        processorArchitecture="*"
                        publicKeyToken="6595b64144ccf1df"
                        language="*"/>
    </dependentAssembly>
  </dependency>
  <compatibility xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:compatibility.v1">
    <application>
      <!-- Windows 10 and 11 -->
      <supportedOS Id="{8e0f7a12-bfb3-4fe8-b9a5-48fd50a15a9a}"/>
    </application>
  </compatibility>
</assembly>
